# Do NOT set BR2_TARGET_ROOTFS_SQUASHFS4_XZ here. U-Boot reads the kernel,
# the device tree, and extlinux.conf out of the squashfs itself, and its
# squashfs driver supports zlib/lzo/lz4/zstd but not xz. Buildroot defaults
# to "-comp gzip", which U-Boot's FS_SQUASHFS handles via ZLIB. Switching to
# xz to save space would produce an image that cannot be booted.
BR2_PACKAGE_CA_CERTIFICATES=y
BR2_PACKAGE_LIBP11=y
BR2_PACKAGE_UNIXODBC=y
BR2_PACKAGE_DTC=y
BR2_PACKAGE_DTC_PROGRAMS=y
BR2_PACKAGE_LIBMNL=y
# Bring-up tools. On-device verification is the unfinished half of this
# system: the button GPIO mapping and the audio routing are inherited from
# mainline's rg35xx-plus.dts on the premise that the RG40XXV is the same
# board family, and nothing here has been confirmed on hardware. evtest and
# aplay/speaker-test are how you confirm or refute that, so they ship in the
# image rather than leaving the README's checklist unfollowable.
BR2_PACKAGE_EVTEST=y
BR2_PACKAGE_ALSA_UTILS=y
BR2_PACKAGE_ALSA_UTILS_APLAY=y
BR2_PACKAGE_ALSA_UTILS_AMIXER=y
BR2_PACKAGE_ALSA_UTILS_SPEAKER_TEST=y

# libdrm's test programs, for the same reason: there is no display support yet,
# but "no picture" covers several unrelated failures and none of them can be
# told apart by looking at the device. modetest enumerates connectors, CRTCs and
# modes and can draw a test pattern, which is what distinguishes "DRM never
# bound" from "bound but dark". See
# docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-12-display-support-plan.md.
#
# No per-GPU option is selected: those build vendor userspace libraries
# (libdrm_intel and friends), while modetest uses the generic KMS ioctls.
BR2_PACKAGE_LIBDRM=y
BR2_PACKAGE_LIBDRM_INSTALL_TESTS=y

# Cairo, for Scenic. scenic_driver_local picks its backend from MIX_TARGET:
# cairo-gtk on the host, cairo-fb under Nerves -- CPU rendering straight into
# /dev/fb0, no GPU involved. Its docs name this exact symbol, and note that
# the official Nerves systems set it by default; this system is not one of
# those, so nothing pulled it in. Without it the driver has nothing to link
# against.
#
# This is deliberately the GPU-independent path. The framebuffer here is
# 640x480 XRGB8888, 1,228,800 bytes a frame, and a full-frame write measured
# 604 fps unthrottled, so CPU-side compositing has ample headroom at this
# size. Anything wanting GLES needs the Mali to bind first -- see
# CONFIG_SUN50I_H6_PRCM_PPU in linux/nerves.fragment.
#
# freetype is not optional and Buildroot will not pull it in on cairo's
# account: the driver's Makefile runs
# `pkg-config --static --libs freetype2 cairo` for both cairo targets, so
# without it the NIF fails to link rather than degrading to no text.
BR2_PACKAGE_CAIRO=y
BR2_PACKAGE_FREETYPE=y

# Mesa, for GLES2 on the Mali-G31. The kernel side works as of
# CONFIG_SUN50I_H6_PRCM_PPU -- panfrost binds and /dev/dri/renderD128 exists --
# but a render node is not an API. Everything that wants the GPU wants EGL and
# GLES2 on top of it: Emerge's DRM/KMS backend requires "OpenGL ES 2 or newer",
# and libretro cores expect the same.
#
# This is not a cheap addition, and the reason is not obvious from the option
# list. Buildroot 2026.05.1 makes the Gallium panfrost driver
# `depends on BR2_PACKAGE_MESA3D_LLVM`, and panfrost also force-selects
# NEEDS_PRECOMP_COMPILER, which pulls in MESA3D_OPENCL, SPIRV_LLVM_TRANSLATOR
# and SPIRV_TOOLS. So asking for panfrost asks for LLVM on the target and a
# SPIR-V toolchain with it. The toolchain prerequisites were already met
# (BR2_INSTALL_LIBSTDCPP, threads, wchar, non-static), so this costs build
# time and disk rather than a toolchain rebuild.
#
# OPENGL_EGL selects GBM on its own; it is listed anyway because kmscube
# depends on the GBM feature symbols directly and a silent deselect here would
# be hard to spot.
# DISABLED, but only just, and no longer for the original reason.
#
# These options need patches/buildroot/0001-mesa3d-panfrost-without-target-llvm
# applied to the Buildroot tree first. Without it, Buildroot's dependency chain
# builds LLVM *for the target* -- ~28.5 GB of build tree -- and the build dies
# with "No space left on device". With it, Mesa compiles and installs
# libEGL.so, libGLESv2.so and the panfrost DRI driver into staging, using
# host-side LLVM only.
#
# What is still unsolved is shipping it rather than building it. The artifact
# step copies the whole host/ tree (13 GB, since staging is a symlink into it)
# and tars it, so peak usage is roughly double. That does not fit in a Docker
# Desktop VM capped at 63 GB (DiskSizeMiB 65536) alongside the build trees.
#
# To enable: apply the patch, raise Docker's disk to ~96 GB, uncomment. The
# toolchain prerequisites were already satisfied and need no changes.
#
# BR2_PACKAGE_MESA3D=y
# BR2_PACKAGE_MESA3D_GALLIUM_DRIVER_PANFROST=y
# BR2_PACKAGE_MESA3D_OPENGL_EGL=y
# BR2_PACKAGE_MESA3D_OPENGL_ES=y
# BR2_PACKAGE_MESA3D_GBM=y

# kmscube is the smallest honest test that the whole stack works: it opens the
# DRM device, creates a GBM surface, brings up EGL, and spins a GLES2 cube on
# the panel. "libEGL.so exists" does not distinguish a working driver from a
# stub, and this does.
# BR2_PACKAGE_KMSCUBE=y
